        "stop_reason": {
          "kind": "finished",
          "message": "Implemented a real INI parser and printer for the repo's dialect, in MoonBit, in `tasks/ini_run_3`.\n\n## What was built\n\nThe `init`-package API required by `ini_spec.mbt` is implemented and nothing else is exposed:\n\n- `@ini.parse(input : StringView) -> Doc raise`\n- `@ini.print(doc : Doc) -> String`\n\nCode is split by responsibility (all root-package `.mbt` files, largest 216 lines):\n\n| file | role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `ini.mbt` | public `parse` entry point |\n| `ini_chars.mbt` | char classes, line splitting (LF/CRLF, BOM), separator / trailing-backslash-run / inline-comment scans |\n| `ini_section.mbt` | `[name]` recognition and name validation |\n| `ini_value.mbt` | the three value carriers, escape table, continuation joining |\n| `ini_parser.mbt` | `Parser` state machine, document assembly |\n| `ini_printer.mbt` | `print`, value/section-name encoding |\n| `ini_error.mbt` | `IniParseError(line, message)` + `reject` |\n| `ini_dialect_test.mbt` | 72 tests derived from `specs/ini.md` |\n\nKey design points: all scanning runs on `Array[Char]` (whole code points, so emoji never split); lines are classified by their first non-blank character (so `[` inside a value is text); the first `=`/`:` wins; inline comments need preceding whitespace; continuation is decided on the **raw** trailing-backslash run before escapes are decoded (odd = marker, even = escaped literals), and continuation lines contribute text, not syntax; document state is just \"the map entries go into\", which makes duplicate keys and re-opened sections last-wins for free. `print` always double-quotes values, and quotes a section name only when the header syntax would otherwise eat it (`a ` → `[\"a \"]`).\n\n## Validation actually run\n\n- `moon test` → **84/84 pass** (12 provided public cases incl. both quickcheck properties, plus 72 new dialect tests: separator precedence, trimming, duplicates, reopen, all four trailing-backslash rows, per-carrier escape tables, comments, quoted values, CRLF, unicode/emoji, invalid-input rejection, error message, hostile-input no-crash, and real-world git/systemd/php.ini samples).\n- `moon fmt` run (output kept for implementation files), `moon info` run (`.mbti` shows exactly `parse`, `print`, `Doc`, `IniParseError`).\n- `moon check` → 0 errors; the only 3 warnings come from the provided fixture files (`ini_spec.mbt`'s `derive(...)`, `ini_pub_test.mbt`'s unqualified `Doc`, `moon.pkg`'s test-only import) — my files are warning-free.\n- An independent review subagent audited the code against `specs/ini.md` with ~85 extra spec-derived assertions; its findings were addressed: `print` now round-trips section names with padding/wrapping quotes, section names blank after quote-unwrapping (`[' ']`) and angle-bracket forms (`[<section>]`) are rejected, and the protected files `ini_spec.mbt`/`ini_qc.mbt` (which `moon fmt` had auto-migrated) were restored to their vendored text — `*_pub_test.mbt`, `ini_qc.mbt`, `ini_spec.mbt` and `specs/` are byte-identical to the fixture.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- `moon.pkg`'s `\"moonbitlang/core/test\"` import is only used by test packages, so `moon check` still reports it as unused; left untouched on purpose so the withheld private test files keep resolving `@test`.\n- Documented dialect limitations in `README.md`: the format cannot express a key with a leading `;`/`#` or edge whitespace, a section name containing `[`/`]`/`<`/`>`, or an empty/whitespace-only section name (noted rather than worked around).\n- `README.md` documents overview, architecture, parsing strategy, usage, testing and design decisions from the actual code."
